#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199077} :activity/id #uuid "da7c19c9-a092-5227-8c3d-c60c670a8222" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048206 :topic/id #uuid "8850e3a0-f2fb-5671-843a-13422a22aa79" :topic/math-academy-id 4387} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199560 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $A$ and $B$ are sets, the Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs $(a,b)$ whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA \\times B = \\big\\{(a, b) \\: : \\: a \\in A,\\, b \\in B \\big\\}\n$$\n\nMany Cartesian products have natural representations in the Cartesian plane. The most common of these is the Cartesian product $\\mathbb R^2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbb R^2 = \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R =  \\big\\{(x, y) \\: : \\: x,y \\in \\mathbb R \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis infinite set contains all ordered pairs $(x,y),$ where $x$ and $y$ can be any real numbers. In other words, $\\mathbb R^2$ contains every point in the Cartesian plane! \n\n![](images/07/070389fcdb6d69f9aa90dabbd98a0d3461e26d3e3e08b1d53b92e99688c09923.png)\n\nSimilarly, the set\n\n$$\n\\mathbb R^3 = \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R =  \\big\\{(x, y, z) \\: : \\: x,y,z \\in \\mathbb R \\big\\}\n$$\n\ndescribes every point in three-dimensional Cartesian space. \n\n![](images/e7/e74fb5948144bc61173e4de73865cb746278bd31fd87e4abf927fa01821ba936.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c18e6618-573b-5560-8d95-58ed56f05155" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8237 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c7652427-e5ff-5d02-9f23-39039f090aff" :step/math-academy-id 153383 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198272 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198169 :question/id #uuid "bb40c174-3739-50df-835e-5d47c9902d5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13192"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050923}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422996}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "2190f1df-5853-5d8c-85e2-33f40f1f0f24" :step/math-academy-id 153384 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199579 :tutorial/content "Cartesian coordinates can also be used to visualize other Cartesian products.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following Cartesian product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^- = \\big\\{(x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in\\mathbb N,\\, y\\in\\mathbb R^-\\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis product contains every point in $\\mathbb R^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative real number.\n\nTo visualize this Cartesian product, consider the following subset of our Cartesian product:\n\n$$\nS_1 = \\big\\{ (1,y) \\: : \\: y \\in\\mathbb{R}^- \\big\\} \\subset \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-\n$$\n\nThis subset describes all points in the plane whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative real number. We can represent $S_1$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f6165f66b4149b7b2ea52a3cd686836853d85048ca890e850cb771174a150c57.png)\n\nNext, we consider the subset $S_2,$ given by\n\n$$\nS_2 = \\big\\{ (2,y) \\: : \\: y \\in\\mathbb{R}^- \\big\\} \\subset \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-.\n$$\n\nThis subset describes all points in the plane whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative real number. \n\n![](images/ca/caf2299a2e8009ad5b2776ce67786fa7c7e3bdb57aacbd7df87882f59a2350e2.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $S_3, S_4, S_5,\\ldots,$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N\\times {R}^{-} & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup ⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-$ as the set of vertical rays shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/03/033c40b86bfd5cebf24d34de644f632213207386c2b67c559af7594468599e1e.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1626e562-8e88-5ff3-bae3-4162438c0086" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8329 :tutorial/title "Cartesian Products of Countably Infinite Sets"} :step/id #uuid "cd371945-c79b-5b78-9017-15c39e4d5ed7" :step/math-academy-id 153387 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199080}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198273 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198154 :question/id #uuid "eae94514-cd92-5405-90b1-ee09dad18e13" :question/math-academy-id "e-13162"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050088}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422995}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "48b1fb46-7071-5897-9dd7-a47e1d955f6c" :step/math-academy-id 153388 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199081}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199584 :tutorial/content "Let's sketch a representation of the set $S= [0,1] \\times [0,1]$ in the Cartesian plane.\n\nThe Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \\big\\{(a,b) \\::\\: a\\in A,\\, b\\in B\\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS = [0,1] \\times [0, 1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS=\n\\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in [0,1],\\, y\\in [0,1] \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider $S_0 \\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals ${\\color{red}0}$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS_{\\color{red}0} = \\big\\{ ({\\color{red}{0}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in [0,1]\\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent $S_{\\color{red}0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/1c/1cc30fcc7543bfbbf8c5c59d1436eb3b1a0799d60c9ad1d899957e8fd17d58ab.png)\n\nNow, consider a general $S_{\\color{blue}{i}} \\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some ${\\color{blue}{i}} \\in [0,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS_{\\color{blue}{i}} = \\big\\{ ({\\color{blue}{i}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in [0,1]\\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor each ${\\color{blue}{i}} \\in [0,1],$ $S_{\\color{blue}{i}}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $({\\color{blue}{i}},0)$ to the point $({\\color{blue}{i}},1).$\n\n![](images/28/28b9aa0559635ed4090f10dd0b59f2cbb6c297f8736d9509a637b5d78d27561c.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $I = [0,1]{:}$\n\n$$\nS = \\bigcup_{{\\color{blue}i} \\in I} S_{\\color{blue}i}.\n$$\n\nAs $\\color{blue}i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following rectangle that represents the union $\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{{\\color{blue}i} \\in I} S_{\\color{blue}i}{:}$\n\n![](images/76/76ff48755dbfc3ba1943665d08aa8cfd00948bbc1ddfaab560a0af64f11bae26.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "923fb377-d694-5181-8561-c57612f15054" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8339 :tutorial/title "The Cartesian Product [0,1] x [0,1]"} :step/id #uuid "ef01039c-9cce-558a-8067-2ffba087e848" :step/math-academy-id 153392 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199082}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198205 :question/id #uuid "aa337681-5d8c-5ea1-b0fe-5d8cd15040a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13320"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390333}] :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "9ddfd97d-7a1a-59ef-8492-3fbe4088f22f" :step/math-academy-id 153393 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198275 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198206 :question/id #uuid "67569060-2f33-5a8d-ae73-690742d3d318" :question/math-academy-id "e-13321"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427501}] :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "5777ec1b-f050-570f-86e4-1e7bffa77dfb" :step/math-academy-id 153396}] :activity/title "Visualizing Cartesian Products" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198532}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427503} :activity/id #uuid "0974eb57-6659-54d9-be86-ddfa7868dbe6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048206 :topic/id #uuid "8850e3a0-f2fb-5671-843a-13422a22aa79" :topic/math-academy-id 4387} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13902432-1e15-5d83-9e31-77ea1c9ecb88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9a/9a87c5ea8ceaa8a64e30b6375a8bcaa58182ac2e97d68b44c70a0a964c4727dd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284963} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc8b5e0-b1db-5812-b923-c8b2aed030ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/34/340f534cb7548f2e5fcffb6153d45fd327b414aa17247bcde00b5396d24830a4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285222} {:answer/id #uuid "95737d26-61bc-5532-92fe-b8144479975c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/32/3274c0747b72b6b8ce457f20cd89d0f7d951c8a3bd138c9061448c1bedb42b55.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286146} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d0322b-eee5-55b7-919c-66200da95a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/82/824d176d4d041be340eb786ff53f8b5a352edca3ad928fc593b9799f2cc4008b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286456} {:answer/id #uuid "ed33c550-8d8c-5c22-8aed-eb90798b7c4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b6/b676b2201a4fe4d57cb005eb9527f429068eae4d18c55496ebabf9390fc19219.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286962}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284963} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc5ac333-7088-55b5-8dff-47ad3124a244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8eb13b5c-5049-574a-9dd7-f6e99016a3c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-182001" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=Z\\times [-1,0]$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times [-1,0]\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a integer number and whose $y$-coordinate belongs to $[-1,0].$\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in [-1,0]\\}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is in $[-1,0].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,0]\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/63/63199d9f35c331339e97ad3b2094a520d1fb6a9892bd0beac83538c43b9e2748.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is in $[-1,0].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/03/03a6a90b3eff572ceed341de55a0d5e625e338ca18f2ae8ee85eed3bb5278167.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times [-1,0]$ as the set of vertical lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/9a/9a87c5ea8ceaa8a64e30b6375a8bcaa58182ac2e97d68b44c70a0a964c4727dd.png)"} :step/id #uuid "2f868252-f8e0-5a5f-92f0-b020aafff313" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427506}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c00cf5df-4240-5b04-850f-1b4b0d6f8ae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c8/c87871c50b343792c73fc18dfe92bc2065b18ed3de1e7bb70324b8b43d027206.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389799} {:answer/id #uuid "1f633a02-8088-56db-88c2-7889ba264321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/39/39db7bc1b259b222a2365441babc3421101961ea26a7143af14b526ba7f81200.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389800} {:answer/id #uuid "6c189e68-bec8-518b-a64d-56fe2a4c4033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ec/ec0e1df36bf5d93b8cdbcc3d14cd510fbe299b929ed3d19f4d901f4461093305.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389801} {:answer/id #uuid "f10813b1-b163-59a5-a38f-0b5b4e7dded5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f9/f90de7c6d88b187af3604912a647620fb88d4833e08fd086cf1a9daae52f331f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389802} {:answer/id #uuid "81b3b685-552f-53ef-8556-c54cf7663b9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/86/8627676ae3b7bcdeff423d79d7289d1d3f9243e0cc69d4f3ac7d3e5c51141fc1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389803}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389800} :answer-field/id #uuid "f376ba00-5dab-590e-a791-4441f99a66cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "60baa69f-bbf2-5905-831d-ab5e879fa2f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-177648" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times [0,1].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,0.5]\\times [0,1]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,0.5],b\\in [0,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(0.5,0),$ $(0.5,1),$ and $(0,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5b/5b882f3fb0e7b44eb2df3a510260574e5c4a4d85842a9c0d65515f1a40894756.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times [0,1]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,i],b\\in [0,1]\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(i,0),$ $(i,1),$ and $(0,1).$\n\n![](images/cb/cb530698591dbd71a8058d9faa0c60b636dc654f21e92d65ec8295aeaa67355a.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $\\{0\\}\\times [0,1]:$\n\n![](images/70/704f8f6f0666d09bb2b8a25eab0548c3eb808e2c9d394d2ae873de35cb0112d1.png)"} :step/id #uuid "49fb4645-c2a9-59e2-8599-72cac0d3d4bc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427504}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427504 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389916 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1204bd88-b470-5b91-b7c2-a8d4ad537898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/46/463ae2387423a48dd557af8cfd9b01b6bc08b95f1b3c2f337c6b0c53d3731fa2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389918} {:answer/id #uuid "60caae02-3071-514b-9258-9f96b6664d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2d/2df87b90d770a5f2c0ba40a55330409d1cd80866b7e34299eab6dcbf675c6a13.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389919} {:answer/id #uuid "746ae67c-caa3-593c-9e3f-51831b036f30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f7/f7e54dd973126e3179e79b1ba1a26f4a7c1c541ca764b14c4ccef73b4452509e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389920} {:answer/id #uuid "c89a44bf-6d27-578e-9136-6babbb95e931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2f/2f28e6f8c8e6e9fa13b5285e25e5460d26548ffd9d71077d1c6073e6c4c743a1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389921} {:answer/id #uuid "51564c2b-bcad-5e6e-9c34-dbd7a16d3a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/58/5888cf137eea674995b4c9cc37285b4130df5de3a2a2fa63b1b5b195910526c1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389922}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389920} :answer-field/id #uuid "c241a74d-c7f4-5b96-a451-f6a50f08f04a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6bae802a-d591-53eb-b628-21b012d03cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-181968" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={Z}^{-}\\times N$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={Z}^{-}\\times N\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a negative integer and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,y\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/8e/8e6816767cd2010fca1b8991ed9c5e0c42d3a6d6466d78025534bc8362a174b9.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{-2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-2}=\\{(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/4d/4d0fe261e790520e6a8786e6f024d5b9cb7cd54c18242103a1f4b77f09653abc.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-5},{S}_{-4},{S}_{-3}$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…{S}_{-4}\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1} \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in {Z}^{-}}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize ${Z}^{-}\\times N$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7e54dd973126e3179e79b1ba1a26f4a7c1c541ca764b14c4ccef73b4452509e.png)"} :step/id #uuid "7ffe2ddd-605f-5cd8-bf11-9de23847ac5f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427505}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a224de-0a7a-5ebf-83b7-7575ba7f49c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f8/f8eb09869ae1733a58ed488cc747bca4dddf56ebc0da0de53ff21a2181208217.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284846} {:answer/id #uuid "72e2efff-69aa-52bb-a78c-e682719225d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/30/30c98beaaaf9728a31685195cd236464ceba34094aeda381838f33ba2a134b4c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285846} {:answer/id #uuid "ca095e46-57a5-5045-9532-8b19c4502e37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/82/8210684df0b5143b3b51a3a30cd03e4cdbc4bb80d139c25385dd0fde668d6fd4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286632} {:answer/id #uuid "d08d3be0-ab3c-598f-b81f-c0a937f9bb66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ee/ee6ac65885afc561a40d51352bb6a4eac4974db27274cb35ad3032b7a7a8ffb4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286686} {:answer/id #uuid "efd1c7f0-4319-573c-83f6-fc4b435b4a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c2/c2474d727fe1d97a21f67e9c4ae71b19dcc98106bb7b34c3ccef19ae56b44487.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286987}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286632} :answer-field/id #uuid "39cb9311-07e3-5d93-89a4-7c78cbd6de5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "10624e27-fb3c-5852-bfb2-5c112258247b" :question/math-academy-id "q-181984" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the set $S=[-1,1]\\times [0,1]$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS=[-1,1]\\times [0,1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in [-1,1],\\,y\\in [0,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}=\\{(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,1]\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/1c/1cc30fcc7543bfbbf8c5c59d1436eb3b1a0799d60c9ad1d899957e8fd17d58ab.png)\n\nNow, consider a general ${S}_{i}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some $i\\in [-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{i}=\\{(i,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,1]\\}\n$$\n\nFor each $i\\in [-1,1],$ ${S}_{i}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,1).$\n\n![](images/49/494c9d77b0ff339b90792270c9d641054a03a2918e3c56a93747b42a77ee3ccf.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $I=[-1,1]:$\n\n$$\nS={⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}.\n$$\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following rectangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/7c/7c5ac5dad0aaacb4347c260d634e6bcd2d6102b25d51813fe106803417cc7539.png)"} :step/id #uuid "8cd3cbad-1220-514c-ad2e-d949f26d97ee" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427502}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b353f4d-b6c1-544c-a565-cbc81d0130e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/49/4928c291f6eb42646dd9623b7c665a1569462b1b5ce19b31501ae3c655303b87.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427495} {:answer/id #uuid "89fe3c93-58d2-564c-8818-f614741ab1a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ff/ffaa9807fa5155dcc848d68bed93a16b267045fae3f5d4d0945e064ad5c11eef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427496} {:answer/id #uuid "99bf3628-2a02-53d8-922c-2471074f7424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0a/0a655218c2ee2a93e2c37601b8d989f449395270fbea8ff2dda34caedccd95e1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427497} {:answer/id #uuid "9a8e4cd5-94f1-51c7-aff4-ee1d59197f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f1/f15dd410cc7b5e47cd246182606ef7e9fb5c51caae792815104ab788d0da9a48.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427498} {:answer/id #uuid "d3489613-d07f-57e1-bd54-7d15cf117eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/bf/bf3963dc09b844714f7cbe85e93caf539b2de64cd6a5264d9a7477e724ea5a8b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427499}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427496} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5e7343a-4633-545d-a3b9-3c2ada585e3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "46ba7fa0-d231-5401-8252-d4e156d84cf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-184533" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times [-1,1].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-1,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(-1,-1),$ $(-1,1),$ $(1,1),$ and $(1,-1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/71/712970edb9966d6544cbe4a360aaa0de3436f356c4c7f23ff601c70ef168a521.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times [-1,1]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-i,i],b\\in [-1,1]\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(-i,-1),$ $(-i,1),$ $(i,1),$ and $(i,-1).$\n\n![](images/68/68f8c51062bd9d3ecadd33b0a67ea0990f631375aba9f9cc705946c79abf47d0.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $\\{0\\}\\times [-1,1]:$\n\n![](images/ff/ffaa9807fa5155dcc848d68bed93a16b267045fae3f5d4d0945e064ad5c11eef.png)"} :step/id #uuid "c27fb01c-6abc-53a8-9ed3-81896b8737cf"}] :activity/title "Visualizing Cartesian Products" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427494}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494058} :activity/id #uuid "4ec125ac-e7e6-5672-8f33-3a74502489fe" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048206 :topic/id #uuid "8850e3a0-f2fb-5671-843a-13422a22aa79" :topic/math-academy-id 4387} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493986 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389895 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63212b76-4dd2-5413-b5ba-4e9195ab76e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/82/82f9ecf98b8b2b4e5f75b3130e8483354c93922dc3a942e271562ac1ad17438e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389897} {:answer/id #uuid "4a858bfb-ee22-5b56-9050-fa66d28f0274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e0/e0fa3e717af02a353db77ae8b357dc338c975166fb5fbe6c5c1309d1c1f511f2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389898} {:answer/id #uuid "7442f1d4-b621-53b9-a885-39076816c0b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c2/c28ba6b0da8364f4e7f157b58827384f9e138a2c0fc53558ce7ab2df2f646747.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389899} {:answer/id #uuid "281cc561-5401-5252-9068-734db096e5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/07/075454dae9ed36967a70537cdf3a3f59931cdd81671ff4059658d2e0df438898.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389900} {:answer/id #uuid "b42501d9-b115-5c79-8921-c1756b26a1c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/29/2922ff5e3a32e01fbc292898c88c1d8eca8d933897d4b9d3c1694b6f5cbeb2a2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389901}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389900} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f377c8c-6598-5676-856c-726dc4aca072" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "734eb671-4f30-5ac2-952a-94e297467ed9" :question/math-academy-id "q-181306" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=[-1,1]\\times \\{-1,1\\}$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=[-1,1]\\times \\{-1,1\\}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is either $-1$ or $1.$\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in [-1,1],\\,y\\in \\{-1,1\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate equals $-1$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(x,-1)\\,:\\,x\\in [-1,1]\\}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3ed05ffc58aad1a6c75e7862c1175f497fb14870e6650cc6c4ce01c3df091d8b.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}=\\{(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in [-1,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/0f/0f57920b2532177b0d64ac3dc95feb6a476bb385112d736d702895862e7de2b2.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the two line segments shown below.\n\n![](images/07/075454dae9ed36967a70537cdf3a3f59931cdd81671ff4059658d2e0df438898.png)"} :step/id #uuid "273024b7-a3c5-551a-be72-b7286c27a144" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494022}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287875 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06ad6e0a-c6f3-535a-b438-cd7ef33e552e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/47/47ee12b24246473a45e057d4da6dac5190a4eab23e18bb37bef494bf07627daa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284841} {:answer/id #uuid "67a76b1b-677d-5667-b8f6-e17c0a0eb5ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/23/236d10f31265b400036e1ddeafa122991bcae1ea878af8b300003fb11acfa85b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285734} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb39817-73c5-556d-a591-eb6ab10a7c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ef/efb6d92ea0dc70fa0cbd9d82ea838b41a4eaab6ee2fc32c6fada9258a4154680.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285956} {:answer/id #uuid "f569f51b-ab90-59de-bd38-4bd9ff476d5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/37/37de777f63941a62b003a4c600a40df648b3d39ce3c1dfc753c6f406fe230330.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287035} {:answer/id #uuid "fa732ef5-7dfb-5052-8ef8-ad4126c8c625" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5e/5e474959f4172f2a3dc7d104a5b7e27a8eece4fc1f13421fefdfdfc2e139c139.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287074}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285734} :answer-field/id #uuid "053ceb7f-15f7-5209-b489-364d6abf0a9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "673cc09b-aa8d-5b71-ab95-ac8a33662caf" :question/math-academy-id "q-184507" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,2]\\times [-i,0].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,2]\\times [-0.5,0]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,2],b\\in [-0.5,0]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(2,0),$ $(2,-0.5),$ and $(0,-0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/58/5837864bfcc40e680cf62b43c0e3d145ba05b1f25e1ccae2f4788902d030d927.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,2]\\times [-i,0]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,2],b\\in [-i,0]\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(2,0),$ $(2,-i),$ and $(0,-i).$\n\n![](images/f0/f0c20436f03db4b0003d087827cefbd76506af423da3f0a3042082b5afeb4b58.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $[0,2]\\times \\{0\\}:$\n\n![](images/23/236d10f31265b400036e1ddeafa122991bcae1ea878af8b300003fb11acfa85b.png)"} :step/id #uuid "631e5e2d-6b28-58a2-bd13-a4dacbfc7d52"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494022 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287749 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f001f66-21ad-5810-9a24-3727a01a58d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/95/95c7144f229cc34fcb98b1c1fa8bda8af8ca9dff68c7ae466e388ce3e525bc85.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285523} {:answer/id #uuid "5829deab-1ed2-5ddf-959a-31d73972126d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ec/eceb9c773d52b8bae73837252a885258b5a9b735cf3f24954b8755049d7cf07d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285596} {:answer/id #uuid "64ba1242-9f38-5065-b2a2-156c1dffa463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/89/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285703} {:answer/id #uuid "6e22185e-2dca-5e48-a97a-c408b9904280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/45/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285800} {:answer/id #uuid "97b4fd05-3101-5cc2-857b-8c7b3c842a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/43/43f665cebf4c3af1f1178d94b4ceec5d60bcfd4e61ebf955ed43cb603f8232b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286171}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285800} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ea3661b-4d1c-56cf-956a-8769e8d2e497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5558c8b0-025f-54bf-b54a-ad58d686884f" :question/math-academy-id "q-181770" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=Z\\times R$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times R\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/3f/3fdfed680e3fb46adbe8a70aac86682aacb85d43e0675923f87658635fc89a1e.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/7f/7fbda500ecca7e18c9db9939ad78d4dcc6bf488bc7fc4de1137e764ce21a0254.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times R$ as the set of vertical lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/45/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png)"} :step/id #uuid "7e3bf0ab-2167-59b1-a2c8-8f2c346337df" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494050}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "146d8845-efd5-5bc8-9da2-601d9879cc45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/66/664f327f59e76cb22e1b9bd0a83f84c24e75ad6a0d176532437f629bf80bfc29.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284969} {:answer/id #uuid "a2618d61-fba9-5cd1-88a1-8d9652f5d74a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/89/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286277} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f95c53-3e3f-565a-9a4b-ea11c7c260f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4d/4ddec65745de14217112f6365d9dd372df5a24a13ef8bedc8db592c9ee18cab1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286281} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e480ca-6b20-5449-ba68-f8ceac3b823c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/05/05323cccad18bb4664fe74112ff312f53ef3e12016be6f7b30ca9146f2ba6119.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286290} {:answer/id #uuid "df934188-ff40-536d-b7a9-b50cdf29ebbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/45/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286828}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286277} :answer-field/id #uuid "0402905b-109e-5a9b-b5e8-b00b20ff4037" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a66eead9-d6a1-5d8c-a676-bee7fbbe7fc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-181777" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=R\\times Z$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=R\\times Z\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a real number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in Z\\}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $-1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(x,-1)\\,:\\,x\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/8e/8e677c814756c4818c25da450d789c974787df60cf4bb18017a96593d3ecc180.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in R\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/f9/f99beeb00c070048ee4ee7c1b6c9e077d7d4e0f3fd9e63aa12b5b58588e14dbb.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times Z & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times Z$ as the set of horizontal lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/89/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png)"} :step/id #uuid "b87742e1-5ccb-50d0-bb27-5711cd8741bd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494010}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494058 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b35462e3-d90d-5229-9bb7-a180bdbea833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/aa/aaa04c2578557d03dd144c27e6a5c223126830ba2a3f8288adc9239daf604e55.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389806} {:answer/id #uuid "32da30c7-4f73-5968-bd10-234d5aa6eb9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/98/98077ef737a2487f9e480b7ffd42376785c5681e2651b98e7b71abb4e645d876.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389807} {:answer/id #uuid "94b8b364-2f47-562b-be4f-8d6d5b53993c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/36/36b36ac05cb1054e198f2e40a2d0ffdd40e952abaa8ddf5c946a38fde946adc8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389808} {:answer/id #uuid "1da4c351-345b-51fc-b9bc-106b693a793a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4a/4a81de1a698eedf6a636d66b68a7c62efa69d1d026a961ae97ab5ecc2ef9828d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389809} {:answer/id #uuid "f684dbe0-2990-5f82-837b-23d70335f3f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/74/749f4fb3a6416147cdd69c08bf806c28e2112929453bb8ae405c48452d82126b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389810}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389809} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a3b75ec-dbba-566e-adfa-fb7b15c6d5b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "62556693-fd48-5895-851c-d4aab5200ee0" :question/math-academy-id "q-177650" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times \\{i\\}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,0.5]\\times \\{0.5\\}=\\{(k,0.5)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,0.5]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(0,0.5)$ to the point $(0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/75/7587daeaea45c5ef3449ecb676133c60bbedb7819308783bac835f3ae9c6883c.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times \\{i\\}=\\{(k,i)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(0,i)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](images/b9/b926204a3d58b25889f1c79dd0f65c1ea990c67bc88e869004396a77970e8dd4.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/4a/4a81de1a698eedf6a636d66b68a7c62efa69d1d026a961ae97ab5ecc2ef9828d.png)"} :step/id #uuid "d95f77bb-e077-5521-bcdf-9c73957e19dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493986}}] :activity/title "Visualizing Cartesian Products" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493374}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180559} :activity/id #uuid "1c5ce268-312c-52a6-9777-1ff390251f96" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048492 :topic/id #uuid "a459a1e3-e019-5c4e-a702-5c6685ca3dec" :topic/math-academy-id 3707} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180559 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180757 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is an affine transformation. The **inverse** of $\\mathbf T$ (if it exists) is an affine transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\\mathbf T^{-1} (\\mathbf T(\\mathbf u)) = \\mathbf T (\\mathbf T^{-1}(\\mathbf u)) = \\mathbf u$$\n\nfor every vector $\\mathbf u\\in\\mathbb R^2.$ In other words, the transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ reverses the action of $\\mathbf T.$\n\nNot all affine transformations are invertible. So how can we determine whether a given affine transformation is invertible?\n\nTo answer this, recall that an affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ can be expressed in matrix form as\n\n$$\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. We can reverse the action of $\\mathbf T$ by making $\\mathbf u$ the subject of this equation, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ {A}^{-1}\\cdot Au & ={A}^{-1}\\cdot (x-b) \\\\ {I}_{2}\\cdot u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b) \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b)\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThis equation gives us a method of computing the inverse of an affine transformation. Additionally, we have the following:\n\n*An affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is invertible if and only if the matrix $A$ that represents the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$ is invertible, that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$*" :tutorial/id #uuid "bf99caa7-1e47-5fea-87ac-9b91151af127" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7697 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "35dfb0c0-efab-5193-92eb-6eb22cc2af9f" :step/math-academy-id 44660 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179770 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179431 :question/id #uuid "f9d3291b-b95b-536b-93ec-9815f7f554d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12428"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e39ae7fc-ff0a-5b97-8e13-1f15b12a07e5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378504}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Invertible Affine Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "30275202-8cd8-51e0-a9e1-92aa287dbe42" :step/math-academy-id 44661 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180758 :tutorial/content "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T,$ expressed in matrix form as\n\n$$\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{\\mathbf x} = \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{A} \\cdot \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{\\mathbf u} + \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{\\mathbf b}.$$\n\nWe wish to determine whether $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ exists and find its matrix form if it does exist.\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get $$\\det(A) = \\left| \\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned} \\right| = 1\\cdot (-2) - (-3) \\cdot 1 = 1 \\neq 0.$$\n\nSince $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTo compute the inverse of $\\mathbf T,$ we will use the formula we derived earlier:\n\n$$\\mathbf{u} = A^{-1}(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})$$\n\nWe start by calculating the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix form of the inverse affine transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c4c88a3e-6514-5b56-ab23-306164b0a3a3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7698 :tutorial/title "Finding the Inverse of an Affine Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "6802d261-21db-5122-886a-1dda8f3a0db4" :step/math-academy-id 44664 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180562}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179771 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179432 :question/id #uuid "af48b8ba-874a-5753-9874-a2b13fc5a65b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12429"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0403251a-9491-5d04-ab5a-ed027024d0a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050944}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378518}] :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Matrix Form"} :step/id #uuid "3f8aecda-4d54-5528-939f-886f4ab93593" :step/math-academy-id 44665 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179772 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179433 :question/id #uuid "c5e70edd-5a39-5e85-9461-68df182780dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12430"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b99071b9-d463-5e3e-9a0d-71711af836cb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406969}] :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Parametric Form"} :step/id #uuid "21d1cffb-5a12-5ee7-ad2a-181e2a8d4474" :step/math-academy-id 44668 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180569}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180568 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179775 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179436 :question/id #uuid "98ebb2b6-475b-53c5-acae-0b1b0f0778d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-12441"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e551b65-4116-58bc-b2bc-e536930c69b4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049254}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406968}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Types of Images Formed by an Affine Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "35abe35f-9e29-539b-aaa5-a2546fd2fbfb" :step/math-academy-id 44704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180569 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180761 :tutorial/content "Consider the square in the $uv$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a8cc0923da86a1a1ac6b666893397263ef3865448080500caa6f17510547d901.png)\n\nWe wish to explore the image of this square under an affine transformation $$\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},$$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors.\n\nSimilar to linear transformations, there are three possibilities depending on the value of $\\det(A)$.\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular ($\\det(A)\\neq 0$). In this case, our square is mapped to a parallelogram.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] + [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane, we get the following parallelogram:\n\n![](images/36/36da067393fa53f4296373d65e3c6177a54d1a901824188687b4a74ea171afdf.png)\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular ($\\det(A)=0$) and $A$ has at least one nonzero entry. In this case, our square is mapped to a line segment.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] + [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane gives the following line segment:\n\n![](images/5e/5e5481e5b2f2218a6097020e260dc991cde05574372c724aa07527458257d94c.png)\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular ($\\det(A)=0$) and $A$ is the zero matrix. In this case, our square is mapped to a point on the plane.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] + [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane gives the following point.\n\n![](images/00/0072c5e06674f79f82e771744fda217e6558830f7a6e4ab387aa9dbf4dda09bc.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "375d6591-08dd-5dbf-b36a-28013839a28b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7706 :tutorial/title "Image Types Formed by Affine Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "5ec4662b-6870-532f-83f9-f7a9cbe5504e" :step/math-academy-id 44705 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180570}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180570 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180762 :tutorial/content "In general, if we consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\Bbb R^2 \\to \\Bbb R^2,$ defined by the mapping\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, then we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases: \n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane (namely, the point with position vector $\\mathbf b$)." :tutorial/id #uuid "560907bc-354a-5693-a8df-e6824454755d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7710 :tutorial/title "A Summary of Image Types"} :step/id #uuid "d71cf118-7383-5aea-a47c-8359fc7dffe4" :step/math-academy-id 44723 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180568}}] :activity/title "The Inverse of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179860}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406970} :activity/id #uuid "13211b98-9cdf-5456-a254-0f2d03a88cbb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048492 :topic/id #uuid "a459a1e3-e019-5c4e-a702-5c6685ca3dec" :topic/math-academy-id 3707} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406970 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259633 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46c52d4e-2631-53e7-8a00-72ab68871a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257401} {:answer/id #uuid "746b1cdb-7c35-5efe-a92c-2cd06400438a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257801} {:answer/id #uuid "a34beb49-2bb7-5107-84ce-cae801ce1c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258211} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2b02b6-f203-59a0-ab8f-6e907c4aac2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258857} {:answer/id #uuid "f75572fb-3d5c-57c1-b45a-ea11a4c996bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258950}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257801} :answer-field/id #uuid "99e69744-50ea-5409-9359-8197e1464b98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "08a12b2e-6f73-5fc0-987c-f4d58e5bc21f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124241" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ -1 & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 8-9\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=\\frac{5}{4}+(-\\frac{9}{4})+2=1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1d9d1d09-adca-5f60-87c8-6eda1712954f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406974}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406971 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fc064fc-557b-51b4-acc4-5e5aac84e9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406956} {:answer/id #uuid "394dd73f-cf48-54c7-81fb-4dd53a787045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406957} {:answer/id #uuid "72653cf4-8cbf-523e-b1d8-e878cfd112ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406961} {:answer/id #uuid "c61e03b3-7008-5b09-9b8a-dd9dc056f91b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406964} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b9f565-9eda-5c15-bc7f-e57e8c6bdef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406957} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eef93f6-f290-530b-8af5-dd127f8894d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "353e50fd-8521-51a5-ae98-273dc887e6d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162221" :question/problem "![](images/99/99b3d68b7f0c89ba430b098f9e7fda95f3013f310060dd71cf86eacc6e51afa7.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 8-(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the quadrilateral under the action of $T$ is a line segment."} :step/id #uuid "57752ad6-299b-53a6-80c6-38f70d7331f1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406972 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1acc367e-71f3-5e97-87ce-92842c5b3257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parallelepiped" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278836} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4b22b4-65fa-5db1-94f1-780f0591f6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parallelogram" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279153} {:answer/id #uuid "638bcae2-f573-5b91-97f0-f5ab7fc66eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279497} {:answer/id #uuid "7e024141-a94e-5f97-ae2f-a04b85d857ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point on the plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279720} {:answer/id #uuid "a42ad425-777b-5f11-8db0-c06039f852ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279497} :answer-field/id #uuid "2222be89-5b86-5d7e-873f-1d11313d8d91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79161a5e-44bb-5845-8a74-564fa863b598" :question/math-academy-id "q-162151" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f8c1c9854ec54dd3caf4487328d3f3c35f2d69680a8fd1c13d28125dac77d03.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the square shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a line segment."} :step/id #uuid "8adcf20b-9143-567f-8916-5a2f8686af25" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406971}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406973 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f998697-e815-585e-b0b5-3820b9ab9a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406958} {:answer/id #uuid "51054506-a98c-59d1-82db-eab8461f2cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406959} {:answer/id #uuid "6c76efec-c230-59b8-bab5-b44500517e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406960} {:answer/id #uuid "80f4eee2-e47a-55c2-b5f0-e42eb70ff849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406962} {:answer/id #uuid "c3375fbb-f890-5cba-a4b4-7f53a73a78cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406963}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406960} :answer-field/id #uuid "20065c9e-4427-527a-a708-a46b3d2f2065" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e92cb69b-a679-593b-8882-9cab8d26a8fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-124289" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=u-v-1,\\,y=3v-2u-6.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=ax+y+9,\\,v=bx+y+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v-1 \\\\ 3v-2u-6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ -2u+3v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x+1 \\\\ y+6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot (x+1)+1\\cdot (y+6) \\\\ 2\\cdot (x+1)+1\\cdot (y+6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3x+y+9 \\\\ 2x+y+8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=3x+y+9,\\,v=2x+y+8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=3+2+8=13.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b7332e33-ee2f-5908-9f85-c258b5d80b5d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406972}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259649 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bed1340-8927-5ff0-ba69-b2b93cdd78e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256896} {:answer/id #uuid "81fb632e-14da-5038-b6f9-74ec0da963a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257937} {:answer/id #uuid "82d7f633-617d-5497-a90c-a0550510c2f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257941} {:answer/id #uuid "d5507f11-f2dc-54ed-aa00-8c211067e5e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258649} {:answer/id #uuid "e3817db8-3192-5a2c-ad51-eb7cb8e7b55c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258649} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa29d767-9911-5a05-a828-3b3c81faca3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f20eb077-8cae-5138-ae0e-248f60ff9fb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-124280" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2-u-v,\\,y=3u+4v-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=7+ax-y,\\,v=bx+y+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2-u-v \\\\ 3u+4v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u-v \\\\ 3u+4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\cdot (x-2)+(-1)\\cdot (y+1) \\\\ 3\\cdot (x-2)+1\\cdot (y+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7-4x-y \\\\ 3x+y-5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=7+(-4)x-y,\\,v=3x+y+(-5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+3+(-5)=-6.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c564b67d-ab6c-5a8c-9962-63cefc9e226d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406973}}] :activity/title "The Inverse of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406955}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412072} :activity/id #uuid "5dadfabb-9118-5878-8b7b-abe67ab51d1a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048492 :topic/id #uuid "a459a1e3-e019-5c4e-a702-5c6685ca3dec" :topic/math-academy-id 3707} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fc064fc-557b-51b4-acc4-5e5aac84e9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406956} {:answer/id #uuid "394dd73f-cf48-54c7-81fb-4dd53a787045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406957} {:answer/id #uuid "72653cf4-8cbf-523e-b1d8-e878cfd112ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406961} {:answer/id #uuid "c61e03b3-7008-5b09-9b8a-dd9dc056f91b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406964} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b9f565-9eda-5c15-bc7f-e57e8c6bdef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406957} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eef93f6-f290-530b-8af5-dd127f8894d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "353e50fd-8521-51a5-ae98-273dc887e6d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162221" :question/problem "![](images/99/99b3d68b7f0c89ba430b098f9e7fda95f3013f310060dd71cf86eacc6e51afa7.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 8-(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the quadrilateral under the action of $T$ is a line segment."} :step/id #uuid "57a42c1b-28d8-57a5-acfd-dd199e88f044"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "411cf09f-aaa8-5213-a15d-e483b24650c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A tetrahedron" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279170} {:answer/id #uuid "87fc0aa9-2d9d-5344-8ce5-d4d954e92fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279807} {:answer/id #uuid "99cffdbb-a38c-5a63-9d04-87167a638bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279951} {:answer/id #uuid "d5f74625-2c51-5fb6-83b9-61fbae0292bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280479} {:answer/id #uuid "e51121c0-fae9-532e-b1aa-79a388ca1f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280613}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279807} :answer-field/id #uuid "aec2e96d-de39-5f66-aab1-77904959b9e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0a795336-4ab0-5101-bbca-aa94f4ca2a93" :question/math-academy-id "q-162214" :question/problem "![](images/00/00151dc7a58722a649a52494434950695a27cb4e7cad14f32f76abbddf629a91.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nx=u-3v+2,\\,y=4v-1.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-3v+2 \\\\ 4v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-3v \\\\ 4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4-0\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible and the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a triangle."} :step/id #uuid "5d41d9ac-1e31-5132-8e1e-f51eb5e05b80" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412068}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23580040-99be-5f75-be43-adefd3cbfade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257077} {:answer/id #uuid "58e9a951-d5aa-5d93-8ae3-c1349449a5e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257554} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc83444-b44c-54bf-83b7-307d0775c0bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258020} {:answer/id #uuid "b1de513c-2a81-54b9-9e95-c28eb012ca2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258331} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c13e37-05bf-5042-b338-1fc4c401c1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258964}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257077} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3cd215d-51f0-56ca-9929-c005182aeb03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6d452c80-d748-5af8-bbed-4f41e3a6ab8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124314" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-3v+2,\\,y=6v-3u-4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+a,\\,v=x+by+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-3v+2 \\\\ 6v-3u-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-3v \\\\ -3u+6v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (x-2)+1\\cdot (y+4) \\\\ 1\\cdot (x-2)+(\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot (y+4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x+y \\\\ x+\\frac{2}{3}y+\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+0,\\,v=x+\\frac{2}{3}y+\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7e79ca9f-a884-5f7c-a928-03d9e0201c9a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259649 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bed1340-8927-5ff0-ba69-b2b93cdd78e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256896} {:answer/id #uuid "81fb632e-14da-5038-b6f9-74ec0da963a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257937} {:answer/id #uuid "82d7f633-617d-5497-a90c-a0550510c2f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257941} {:answer/id #uuid "d5507f11-f2dc-54ed-aa00-8c211067e5e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258649} {:answer/id #uuid "e3817db8-3192-5a2c-ad51-eb7cb8e7b55c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258649} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa29d767-9911-5a05-a828-3b3c81faca3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f20eb077-8cae-5138-ae0e-248f60ff9fb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-124280" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2-u-v,\\,y=3u+4v-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=7+ax-y,\\,v=bx+y+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2-u-v \\\\ 3u+4v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u-v \\\\ 3u+4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\cdot (x-2)+(-1)\\cdot (y+1) \\\\ 3\\cdot (x-2)+1\\cdot (y+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7-4x-y \\\\ 3x+y-5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=7+(-4)x-y,\\,v=3x+y+(-5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+3+(-5)=-6.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d9eae084-1f11-516b-9c30-618279f69755" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412072 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179995 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0086e1d8-f0ee-53c6-8443-35f56170d9d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256787} {:answer/id #uuid "0d67fff4-252d-5baa-b919-6e2e71d71094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256912} {:answer/id #uuid "80d9c10d-d8ce-5ef8-9f6d-73c22d791bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257926} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f2b7b2-0f28-5166-96d2-007456d5faee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258602} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a8a822-04f8-5fb6-8bb9-09b853fcf229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258608}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258602} :answer-field/id #uuid "79824dff-905f-590c-af91-7c9745e11bfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "24203ad0-c666-584c-ace2-35e7e7f927ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-124117" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-2+1+(-8)=-9.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e391a44c-ebaa-5dea-a1f8-4caf64dd590c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412071}}] :activity/title "The Inverse of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412067}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435192} :activity/id #uuid "fe41318c-94a2-58c6-a22a-4e621881d521" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048492 :topic/id #uuid "a459a1e3-e019-5c4e-a702-5c6685ca3dec" :topic/math-academy-id 3707} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435191 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259634 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4892ddc7-1f21-5fb6-9203-29d772bde8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257419} {:answer/id #uuid "92a426f4-8720-57e6-a1e8-ad581c1828da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258075} {:answer/id #uuid "9f843863-e410-57ac-ad2a-c4d41c982c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258183} {:answer/id #uuid "a1613b31-d49f-5ba6-a356-2221335e1c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258196} {:answer/id #uuid "b9fab22d-ecf7-5ea8-a60c-5286e5e43015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258411} :answer-field/id #uuid "da74548d-3633-5796-b2bf-ea07c5e41132" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8b41b10b-145c-5e96-a5f6-3186f40b3311" :question/math-academy-id "q-124244" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & 5 \\\\ b & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-3+(-1)+8=4.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1b672460-44db-5bc9-bbbb-d13ca12e5373" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435193}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435192 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "709e3e28-43f0-5774-9967-f2a77296b518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257772} {:answer/id #uuid "7a5307ee-7945-53c6-8b5c-e3c7b40d7f5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257864} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a72ca3-839f-596e-9ba3-db4dee4a50f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258642} {:answer/id #uuid "deb6b58c-00ac-51db-9e70-4039e8ace72e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258721} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ff2522-63f2-5ecd-8947-0cdfd4cb6b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258792}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258721} :answer-field/id #uuid "fed49f22-b423-5b9a-b990-ba7eb27d6825" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c388fe2c-958c-51fb-bcca-cd64f6bc965e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124224" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ a & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-1\\cdot (-9)-2\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+(-9)+20=7.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "55933838-8190-5662-8692-814c8a886119" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435191}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435193 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23580040-99be-5f75-be43-adefd3cbfade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257077} {:answer/id #uuid "58e9a951-d5aa-5d93-8ae3-c1349449a5e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257554} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc83444-b44c-54bf-83b7-307d0775c0bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258020} {:answer/id #uuid "b1de513c-2a81-54b9-9e95-c28eb012ca2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258331} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c13e37-05bf-5042-b338-1fc4c401c1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258964}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257077} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3cd215d-51f0-56ca-9929-c005182aeb03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6d452c80-d748-5af8-bbed-4f41e3a6ab8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124314" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-3v+2,\\,y=6v-3u-4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+a,\\,v=x+by+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-3v+2 \\\\ 6v-3u-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-3v \\\\ -3u+6v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (x-2)+1\\cdot (y+4) \\\\ 1\\cdot (x-2)+(\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot (y+4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x+y \\\\ x+\\frac{2}{3}y+\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+0,\\,v=x+\\frac{2}{3}y+\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7c2cc732-641c-57e5-95b3-542bab8f0800"}] :activity/title "The Inverse of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435190}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445237} :activity/id #uuid "8d550678-9055-5adb-a6ff-5b7c64c11448" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048492 :topic/id #uuid "a459a1e3-e019-5c4e-a702-5c6685ca3dec" :topic/math-academy-id 3707} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445235 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259660 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e2f4802-202c-5744-af7c-a265e1282f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257190} {:answer/id #uuid "6145b310-d9fa-5c70-bf9e-37a7eee79a20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257636} {:answer/id #uuid "6565c78d-4700-5b5e-8fa6-fa3c4fceeb2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257675} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7de5ee-ba45-55a9-a92f-e771483fbdc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257917} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c4affd-1411-548e-b075-bcc64b41f0e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257190} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ef3bd3-deb2-573a-a0a0-ddce62489c56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2c660cc6-fa1c-504a-b604-d474fb35da3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124315" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=5u-9v-2,\\,y=4u-8v+4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=ax-\\frac{9}{4}y+b,\\,v=cx-\\frac{5}{4}y+7.\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5u-9v-2 \\\\ 4u-8v+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5u-9v \\\\ 4u-8v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}5 & -9 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-8\\cdot 5-(-9)\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-4}[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x+2 \\\\ y-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (x+2)-\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot (y-4) \\\\ 1\\cdot (x+2)+(-\\frac{5}{4})\\cdot (y-4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{9}{4}y+13 \\\\ x-\\frac{5}{4}y+7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x-\\frac{9}{4}y+13,\\,v=1x-\\frac{5}{4}y+7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+13+1=16.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1e5edc5f-97f4-5214-8e0a-72cb196631dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445236}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445236 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1acc367e-71f3-5e97-87ce-92842c5b3257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parallelepiped" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278836} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4b22b4-65fa-5db1-94f1-780f0591f6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parallelogram" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279153} {:answer/id #uuid "638bcae2-f573-5b91-97f0-f5ab7fc66eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279497} {:answer/id #uuid "7e024141-a94e-5f97-ae2f-a04b85d857ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point on the plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279720} {:answer/id #uuid "a42ad425-777b-5f11-8db0-c06039f852ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279497} :answer-field/id #uuid "2222be89-5b86-5d7e-873f-1d11313d8d91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79161a5e-44bb-5845-8a74-564fa863b598" :question/math-academy-id "q-162151" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f8c1c9854ec54dd3caf4487328d3f3c35f2d69680a8fd1c13d28125dac77d03.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the square shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a line segment."} :step/id #uuid "31efd4db-96ca-5899-a79c-9be17347f5b5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445239}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259626 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a8b2e4c-71f4-525e-882c-30ff8c65e625" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257299} {:answer/id #uuid "6713e9ff-c15a-5839-bf29-fa79fff395dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257695} {:answer/id #uuid "6d6d52a8-e329-53f2-9470-69f201f764c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257744} {:answer/id #uuid "db338955-c82e-5393-9733-8fbd662c8303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258696} {:answer/id #uuid "dc799e2e-0aae-57e8-a311-8b305ba143a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258703}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257744} :answer-field/id #uuid "837832a8-e75c-560a-ae2c-b1843afa6adc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "51a6b16d-03c2-54f1-bf60-c27ba81220b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-124228" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & -2 \\\\ -4 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-4)}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-7+(-1)+3=-5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "320920d1-2f71-59ff-899e-943cde1b5fe3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445238}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445238 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04abb601-17e0-5fec-b990-97706ac5a596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256822} {:answer/id #uuid "20b7769d-6be5-53ae-a10f-0d9f4c90d0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257049} {:answer/id #uuid "525f19b0-d029-5095-845b-fc54069a98b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257485} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffe1822-1c33-543b-8781-69059dae9e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257765} {:answer/id #uuid "97f74daa-bcde-5c29-bc67-733be230ca01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258121}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257049} :answer-field/id #uuid "a412914a-2d2e-597f-8fb0-48dccc9cd9de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ab7706f1-5139-5e95-9482-c097bef51185" :question/math-academy-id "q-124282" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-v+1,\\,y=9u-4v+3.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=y-4x+a,\\,v=by+cx+3,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-v+1 \\\\ 9u-4v+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-v \\\\ 9u-4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 9 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-4\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 9}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\cdot (x-1)+1\\cdot (y-3) \\\\ -9\\cdot (x-1)+2\\cdot (y-3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}y-4x+1 \\\\ 2y-9x+3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=y-4x+1,\\,v=2y+(-9)x+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+2+(-9)=-6.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "33e812b7-0a19-5479-b824-948d87ff1605" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445235}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445239 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389257 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e04b870d-e25b-5d96-b606-28a110148002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389259} {:answer/id #uuid "062427a9-0f48-53fc-94f1-c6c3d5e866d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389260} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b5ad5a-830d-59fb-be20-4b48087915f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389261} {:answer/id #uuid "4003098a-783e-5e93-bb25-5f94f36652a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389262} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7e5e40-3f3f-5c2d-abac-4a9137d31956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389263}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389262} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1250926-eea3-55ee-b530-0094a4347a99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b31d6cdc-6f82-507f-838b-9eddf7b3f11a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162215" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f550963f047a3860566564abc2cb1e9f3939125895912762ff751767ca55c57.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nx=2u-v,\\,y=4u-v+2.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the pentagon shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-v \\\\ 4u-v+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-v \\\\ 4u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible, and the image of the pentagon under the action of $T$ is a pentagon."} :step/id #uuid "86b72ad4-07e7-5b0a-8c51-311053773e89"}] :activity/title "The Inverse of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445234}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125332} :activity/id #uuid "987e1e0c-bea1-5c9c-84ad-c50c09fe4abb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049723 :topic/id #uuid "0e15397e-6c32-508c-b6ea-90f1a9e5a237" :topic/math-academy-id 1904} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125332 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125757 :tutorial/content "The **pivots** of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nFor example, given the matrix \n![](images/ad/addf5cd1e1219a1e1ce28f3d27d1b000b3a99640ff89a62e55b66f3a5cdb3521.svg)\nwhich is in row echelon form, let's find the pivots of $A.$\n\n- The first non-zero entry in the first row is the entry $a_{11}=1.$ So this is a pivot.\n\n- The first non-zero entry of the second row is $a_{22}=1.$ So this is also a pivot.\n\n- The first non-zero entry in the third row is $a_{34}=1.$ Therefore this is a pivot.\n\nThe pivots of $A$ are highlighted below.\n\n![](images/96/96dcf8cf9f0d387e0539ea04dd7ae4a498534361ad6bd5ed3bb119ae21f0416a.svg)\n\nThe **pivot columns** of a matrix in row echelon form are simply the columns in which the pivots appear.\n\nFor our matrix $A$, we can see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns.\n\nThe non-pivot columns are the columns that do not contain a pivot. So for the matrix $A,$ the only non-pivot column is the $3$rd column." :tutorial/id #uuid "feec6418-f447-5ee0-8c62-7d6b690f9228" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3157 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a9d24104-f86a-5d95-8475-56e2dbec3b1c" :step/math-academy-id 17200 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125333}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125333 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124477 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124290 :question/id #uuid "ccfdc303-534f-530e-810b-f475a997f5d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4703"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "488c9762-554b-5ea6-b414-ce6cbde9bbd1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048980}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414496}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix in Row Echelon Form"} :step/id #uuid "791285ec-5594-542a-8408-63f1e171f030" :step/math-academy-id 17201 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125334}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125334 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124478 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124291 :question/id #uuid "22a77802-4114-516f-a0e0-4cda2e069361" :question/math-academy-id "e-4704"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5f0d2e9-1cf6-5fe4-85cc-f401382f39b2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414497}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Non-Pivot Columns of a Matrix in Row Echelon Form"} :step/id #uuid "a8ae7cc9-0379-5556-914f-78c73a269672" :step/math-academy-id 17202 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125335}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125335 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125758 :tutorial/content "To find the pivots and pivot columns of a general matrix, we first need to convert the matrix to row echelon form (REF).\n\n- The pivots of any matrix are the pivots of its row equivalent REF matrix.\n\n- The pivot columns of a matrix are the pivot columns of its row equivalent REF matrix.\n\nTo reduce a general $n\\times m$ matrix to row echelon form, we follow a similar procedure to the $2\\times 2$ and $3\\times 3$ cases, as we'll see in the next example." :tutorial/id #uuid "d45a4eba-96ce-5bef-92b7-b8f0d4cd8b72" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3158 :tutorial/title "Pivot Columns of a General Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "c86295bd-d79e-5f83-97d7-9a828e1b01e3" :step/math-academy-id 17203 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125336}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125336 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124479 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124292 :question/id #uuid "6e4b2e85-86a8-5050-a60a-ae8d40453ad0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4705"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1073953e-7e0a-5237-8533-8882b7772741" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049120}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124884} {:db/id #edb/ref 1